<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chōsokabe Morichika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1575">1575</a>–<a href="/years/1615">1615</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Chosokabe Morichika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chōsokabe Morichika (1575–1615) was the last Chōsokabe lord of Tosa, a son of Motochika. He joined the western army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600, and after its defeat lost his domain, which passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a>. Living as a teacher in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, he joined the defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> in 1614–15; after its fall he was captured and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika.jpg" alt="Chōsokabe Morichika" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suzuki Shōnen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%C5%8Dsokabe_Morichika.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Morichika, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Azuchi–Momoyama and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo periods</a> whose childhood name was Senkumamaru, was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chōsokabe Motochika</a> and Lady Motochika, who died in 1583. He was named heir to the Chōsokabe after the death of his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-nobuchika">Chōsokabe Nobuchika</a> in 1587, and he married Nobuchika's daughter. He fought in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> in 1590 and in the first of the Korean campaigns, in 1592–93. His father died in 1599.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1600 Morichika sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> and commanded 6,600 men at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>, where his troops faced those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-terumasa">Ikeda Terumasa</a>. Through failures of communication, the positions they held and the betrayal of neighbouring armies, his forces stood idle and saw almost no action before the Western Army collapsed. That same year he ordered the execution of his elder brother Tsuno Chikatada, who had questioned his right to succeed Motochika as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa Province</a>. Although he sent an apology to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, he was deprived of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1614 Morichika went to join the defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> against the Tokugawa, arriving on the same day as Sanada Yukimura, and his Chōsokabe contingent fought very well in both the Winter and the Summer campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> and Chōsokabe forces at the Battle of Tennōji and the fall of Osaka, he tried to flee but was caught at Hachiman-yama by men of the Hachisuka. He and his sons were beheaded on 11 May 1615. His four sons, Moritaka, Morisada, Morinobu and Moritsune, all died that year, as did Moritsune's son Moritane.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chōsokabe Morichika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Morichika</b> joined the western army at Sekigahara in 1600 and lost Tosa, which passed to Yamauchi Kazutoyo as the Tosa Domain.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Chosokabe Morichika</b> lost Tosa for joining the western army at Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave the province to Yamauchi Kazutoyo, who arrived in 1601 and built Kochi castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tosa-domain">Tosa Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Rokujō Riverbed. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.75,133.5&amp;year=1601" aria-label="Shikoku on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7956 940 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chōsokabe Morichika"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8406 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8406 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.75,133.5&amp;year=1601">What was here in 1601? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
